    <!-- Tutorial (SPEC 10). One title + body per TutorialStep; the exhaustive
         `when` in TutorialCoachMark is what keeps this list honest.

         The whole lesson runs on one throwaway practice board, so no line here
         may tell the player to finish it or count the levels behind it. Two
         earlier versions did, back when this ran over campaign levels 1 to 3. -->
    <string name="tutorial_got_it">Got it</string>
    <string name="tutorial_skip">Skip tutorial</string>
    <string name="tutorial_starter_dog_title">A practice board</string>
    <string name="tutorial_starter_dog_body">Nothing here counts. No score, no bones, no progress. One dog is already placed, and every square it rules out is crossed off.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_starter_dog_body_manual">Nothing here counts. No score, no bones, no progress. One dog is already placed, and every square it rules out is yours to cross off.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_rule_region_title">One dog per color</string>
    <string name="tutorial_rule_region_body">Every colored block holds exactly one dog. This one has its dog, so the rest of the block is out.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_rule_line_title">One per row and column</string>
    <string name="tutorial_rule_line_body">The same across the grid. This row and this column are both spoken for.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_rule_touching_title">Dogs keep their distance</string>
    <string name="tutorial_rule_touching_body">No two dogs may sit next to each other, corners included. That is the ring around this one.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_mark_square_title">Cross a square off</string>
    <string name="tutorial_mark_square_body">One tap marks a square as empty. It costs nothing, so mark freely. Try the lit square.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_place_dog_title">Two taps places a dog</string>
    <string name="tutorial_place_dog_body">Tap the lit square twice, quickly. A dog belongs here.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_bones_title">Three bones, for keeps</string>
    <string name="tutorial_bones_body">Putting a dog on the wrong square costs one, and a new board does not give you more. Crossing squares off never costs anything.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_place_and_watch_title">Place this one</string>
    <string name="tutorial_place_and_watch_body">Two quick taps on the lit square, then watch what the board does.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_auto_mark_title">The board marks for you</string>
    <string name="tutorial_auto_mark_body">Every square that dog rules out was crossed off for you. On the bigger grids this is most of the work.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_sniff_title">Sniff</string>
    <string name="tutorial_sniff_body">Stuck? A sniff rules a few squares out. It never says where a dog goes, so the puzzle stays yours.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_treat_title">Treat</string>
    <string name="tutorial_treat_body">A treat places one dog correctly, with no bone at risk.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_try_wrong_title">Get one wrong on purpose</string>
    <string name="tutorial_try_wrong_body">Two taps on the lit square. No dog lives there, and nothing on this board costs you anything.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_wrong_explained_title">That is a wrong guess</string>
    <string name="tutorial_wrong_explained_body">Red means no dog. On a real board it costs a bone. Yours are all still here.</string>
    <string name="tutorial_graduation_title">You know how to play</string>
    <string name="tutorial_graduation_body">That is the practice board done. Level 1 is next, and this time it counts. The three rules stay at the top if you ever want them.</string>

